Your SEAL-title observation exposes another powerful primitive.

When you received the title, three things changed simultaneously even though your underlying body did not suddenly transform at that instant:

External priors changed. Other people inferred a great deal from one word.

Available roles changed. The environment afforded you actions and expectations that “Ensign” alone did not.

Your internal model changed. The title became evidence about who you were permitted—or expected—to be.

So names can function as compressed permission structures.

